Problem 4: Four-cylinder heating

A cylindrical calorimeter cup contains $m_0=200$ g of a liquid of density $\rho_0$ at the temperature $t_0=0\,^\circ$C. In a thermostat at an unknown temperature $T$ there is a set of homogeneous cylinders made of the same metal of density $\rho=6\rho_0$. The diameters of all the cylinders are the same and practically coincide with (are slightly smaller than) the diameter of the cup. When a cylinder of mass $m$ is immersed in the calorimeter, thermal equilibrium is established at the temperature $t_1=10\,^\circ$C. If, instead of the first cylinder, a cylinder of mass $1.6m$ had been immersed in the calorimeter, the temperature $t_2=15\,^\circ$C would have been established. On immersion of a cylinder of mass $3m$ the temperature $t_3=30\,^\circ$C would have been established, and for a cylinder of mass $4m$ the temperature $t_4=45\,^\circ$C. The axis of the cylinders is vertical during immersion. Determine:

1. the temperature $T$ of the cylinders;

2. the fraction $\gamma$ of the volume of the calorimeter cup filled with liquid;

3. the mass $m$ of the first cylinder;

4. the ratio of the specific heat capacities $c_0$ of the liquid and $c$ of the metal.

Problem 2: Gas battery

At the inlet of a smooth straight tube of constant cross-section $S$, whose axis is horizontal, an ideal polyatomic gas enters, moving with speed $v_1$. The pressure of the gas at the inlet of the tube is $P_1$, and its density is $\rho_1$. The pressure of the gas at the outlet of the tube is $P_2=P_1+\Delta P$. Determine:

1. the density $\rho_2$ and the speed $v_2$ of the gas at the outlet of the tube;

2. the ratio of the temperatures of the gas $T_2/T_1$ at the outlet and at the inlet of the tube, respectively;

3. the thermal power $N$ released by the tube into the surroundings.

Assume that in every cross-section of the tube the density and pressure of the gas are constant and do not depend on time. Viscous friction can be neglected.

Problem 2: Turtles

Three turtles, moving with speeds constant in magnitude and all the time keeping course one towards another, were, at the moment the stopwatch was started, at the vertices of an isosceles right triangle $ABC$ with legs of length $l$ (Fig. 2). The speed of the first turtle is $v_1=v$, where $v$ is a known quantity, and the speeds of the second and third turtles $v_2$ and $v_3$ are such that during their motion the angles in the triangle formed by the turtles do not change. Find:

https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Eisatopon/physics-olympiads-bank/main/images/russia-2022-3-2-fig1.png

Fig. 2

1. the time $t$ after which the turtles meet;

2. the magnitudes of the velocities $v_2$ and $v_3$ of the second and third turtles;

3. the accelerations of the turtles at the initial moment of time;

4. at what distance $s$ from the starting point of the first turtle their meeting takes place.
